{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>A journey of seven days.</b> There is a fine point to examine: why was Jacob different in this journey, that the earth did not contract for him as is the way for the lovers of His name — and we find that even for Eliezer, the servant of his father, the earth contracted (Genesis Rabbah 59:11) — and it would have been fitting and proper to do so also to save him from the pursuer, so that Laban would not have reached him? Or perhaps, because the Master, blessed be He, indicated to him in this matter that there was no need to flee, for even though Laban caught up with him, he was unable to say even one word to him — and had the earth contracted, it would have shown, heaven forbid, that He could not save him from Laban's hand. And great is this miracle, that although he [Laban] possessed free choice, he was prevented from exercising his free choice against Jacob." "<b>Also</b> in this, HaShem orchestrated several sequences of events leading to the making of a covenant with him [Laban] not to harm — for through the agency of this very matter, Israel were overcome in the days of the Judges when he came upon them, and he is Cushan-Rishathaim, as they said, of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 105a). Also from this there followed the burial of Rachel in that place, so that she would serve as a sign for the good of her children in their exile, as they said, of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 82), regarding the reason for her burial there:"], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:45:17.367698287Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}